(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-08"] = {"1.1":{"s":"A composite function is a function of a function, such as y = f(u) with u = g(x). Its derivative is found by the chain rule: differentiate the outer function at the inner one, then multiply by the derivative of the inner function. The rule extends to any number of links in the chain.","f":[{"x":"dy/dx = (dy/du)*(du/dx)","n":"Chain rule; equivalently d/dx f(g(x)) = f'(g(x))*g'(x).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.3"}],"w":["Do not forget the inner derivative: d/dx sin(x²) = 2x cos(x²), not cos(x²)."]},"1.2":{"s":"The derivative of f at x = a is the limit of the slope of the secant through (a, f(a)) and a nearby point as that point slides towards it. So f′(a) is the slope of the tangent to the curve at that point. This is the link between derivatives and tangents used later for equations of tangents and normals.","f":[{"x":"f'(a) = lim(h -> 0) (f(a + h) - f(a))/h","n":"Slope of the tangent at x = a, when the limit exists.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.13"}]},"1.3":{"s":"If y = f(x) has an inverse x = f⁻¹(y), the derivative of the inverse is the reciprocal of the original derivative, wherever that derivative is not zero. This gives the derivatives of the inverse trigonometric functions, valid on their principal branches, for example the derivative of sin⁻¹x is 1/√(1 − x²) for |x| < 1.","f":[{"x":"dx/dy = 1/(dy/dx)","n":"Derivative of the inverse function, where dy/dx != 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.15"},{"x":"d/dx sin^-1(x) = 1/sqrt(1 - x^2)","n":"For |x| < 1; for cos^-1(x) the same with a minus sign.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.17"}],"w":["d/dx tan⁻¹x = 1/(1 + x²) for all real x; cot⁻¹x has the minus sign."]},"1.4":{"s":"For products, quotients and powers, especially a function raised to a function, take the natural logarithm of both sides first. The log laws turn products into sums and powers into products, so differentiating becomes easy. Then multiply back by y to get dy/dx.","w":["For a function raised to a function, such as xˣ, neither the power rule nor the exponential rule alone works; take logs first.","After differentiating log y you get (1/y) dy/dx: remember to multiply by y."]},"1.5":{"s":"When x and y are linked by an equation that is not solved for y (an implicit function), differentiate both sides with respect to x, treating y as a function of x so every y-term picks up a factor dy/dx. Then collect the dy/dx terms on one side and solve for it.","w":["d/dx (y²) = 2y dy/dx, not 2y; d/dx (xy) needs the product rule."]},"1.6":{"s":"When x and y are both given in terms of a parameter t, dy/dx is the ratio of their derivatives with respect to t, provided dx/dt is not zero. For the second derivative, differentiate dy/dx with respect to t and then divide again by dx/dt.","f":[{"x":"dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt)","n":"Derivative of a parametric function, where dx/dt != 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.41"}],"w":["d²y/dx² is NOT (d²y/dt²)/(d²x/dt²); differentiate dy/dx w.r.t. t, then divide by dx/dt."]},"1.7":{"s":"The derivative of f′(x) is the second derivative f″(x), written d²y/dx²; differentiating again gives the third and higher derivatives. They measure how the rate of change itself changes, for example acceleration as the second derivative of displacement, and are used in the second derivative test for maxima and minima.","f":[{"x":"d^2y/dx^2 = d/dx(dy/dx)","n":"Second derivative; the n-th derivative is written d^n y/dx^n.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.49"}]}};
